<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julien Fédon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1796">1796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julien Fédon was a free coloured planter of French descent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>. Inspired by revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he led the rebellion of 1795 to 1796, which won the support of enslaved people, held most of the island and killed the British lieutenant governor and other hostages. British troops crushed the revolt in 1796, and Fédon was never captured.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julien-fedon.jpg" alt="Julien Fédon" width="233" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julien-Fedon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fédon was born on Martinique, the son of Pierre Fédon, a jeweller who had come there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> in 1749, and of a freed black woman. The family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> in the 1750s, while the island was still French, though the historian Edward Cox found that Fédon himself seems not to have lived there until 1772. In 1787 he married Marie Rose Cavelan, a woman of mixed race, and they settled on the Belvedere Estate in Saint John Parish. Many French-speakers on the island, Catholics and people of mixed race above all, had limited rights under the British, who had taken Grenada in 1763.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The revolt began on the night of 2 March 1795 with attacks on Grenville, where the rebels looted, burned houses and killed many British planters, and Gouyave, where they took white captives, among them Ninian Home. Back at Belvedere they were joined by large numbers of slaves who had fled the plantations, and Fédon fortified the mountains. About 14,000 of the island's 28,000 slaves sided with the rebels, and some 7,000 of them were killed.</p><p>When Fédon's brother died in the fighting at Belvedere on 8 April 1795, Fédon had 48 of his 53 British prisoners executed, Home among them, as he had threatened to do if his camp were attacked. The rebels held the whole island except St. George Parish, the seat of government. Historians see their failed attack on St. George as the cause of their defeat; the next day they were beaten near Mount Qua Qua.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Fédon drew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, the revolutionary leaders of Guadeloupe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>; some historians hold that he meant to free the slaves, others that he wanted only to return the island to Catholic French rule. Some believe he drowned fleeing by canoe, others that he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, and sightings were claimed as late as 1815. In the 19th and 20th centuries he was regarded as a folk hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>. The mountain that was his base, once Morne Vauclain, is now Morne Fédon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julien Fédon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1795 to 1796 <b>Julien Fédon</b>, a free coloured planter inspired by revolutionary France, led a rebellion that held most of the island and killed the lieutenant governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-grenada">Colony of Grenada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Martinique, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.65,-61.02&amp;year=1795" aria-label="Martinique on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Julien Fédon"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4916 3761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4916 3761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.65,-61.02&amp;year=1795">What was here in 1795? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
